# STANDING REMINDERS (read every session; do not skip)

**COPY RULE: NO EM DASHES.** Never write an em dash (the long dash character) in any copy you author: UI strings, comments, docs, commit messages, PR text, plans, or chat replies. Use a period, a comma, a colon, parentheses, or a hyphenated phrase instead. If you catch yourself typing one, rewrite the sentence. Existing docs may still contain them; do not introduce new ones.

**LEGAL DRAFTS: UPDATE ON EVERY BUILD.** `guide-docs/docs/PRIVACY.md` and `guide-docs/docs/TERMS.md` are living master drafts for the real Privacy Policy and Terms / EULA. Any task that collects data, changes how data is used, adds a permission, payment, analytics SDK, AI touchpoint, UGC surface, moderation path, account/deletion flow, or age gate **must** add or update the matching bullet in those two files **in the same change**. A feature is not done if PRIVACY/TERMS were skipped. Fill gaps for already-shipped work when you touch that area.

These rules apply to every task, every file, every session. Re-read this file at the start of each session. When any rule conflicts with an ad-hoc instruction in a prompt, ask before proceeding. When code and docs disagree, the docs in `guide-docs/` win (see doc authority at the bottom).

1 · The stack is decided. Do not re-litigate it.
The tech stack, hosting, and deployment are already chosen and specified in INFRASTRUCTURE.md. Read it before any infra, build, or dependency work — every time.
In short: one Expo (+ Expo Router) codebase → iOS + Android + web · NestJS API in a container on AWS App Runner · Supabase (Postgres + RLS + pgvector + Auth + Storage) · Anthropic + OpenAI server-side only · IaC via Terraform/CDK · CI via GitHub Actions + EAS.
Never introduce a new database, hosting provider, state library, or major dependency without an explicit go-ahead. No blockchain — ever (the reasons are in INFRASTRUCTURE.md).
Secrets (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, Supabase service keys) exist only server-side (Secrets Manager). If you find yourself writing a key into client code, stop — that's a bug.

2 · UI comes from the Magic Patterns component library. Always.
The UI was designed and built in Magic Patterns; you are connected to that component library. Whenever you build or edit UI, use the Magic Patterns components — don't hand-roll a lookalike, don't reach for a random UI kit, don't invent a new button.
The component inventory and the doc each component belongs to are listed in MAGIC-PATTERNS.md. Screens must match their spec docs (HOME.md, PROFILE.md, etc.).
If a needed component doesn't exist in the library, flag it (so it gets designed in Magic Patterns) rather than improvising — a temporary placeholder is fine if it's clearly marked // TODO: replace with Magic Patterns component <Name>.
Visual rules live in DESIGN.md (eggshell/black canvas, pixel headers, beveled metallic primaries, floating pill nav, no gradients/shadows, motion behind prefers-reduced-motion). Never override tokens locally.
3 · Accessibility & app-store compliance are built in from day one. This is crucial.

Building this early is far cheaper than retrofitting, and store review will block launch without it.

Accessibility (target WCAG 2.2 AA):

Every interactive element gets an accessible label/role (accessibilityLabel, accessibilityRole in RN; semantic HTML + ARIA on web). Test flows with VoiceOver / TalkBack in mind.
Contrast ≥ 4.5:1 for text (this is why "View original" exists on customized profiles — never break it).
Tap targets ≥ 44×44pt. Support Dynamic Type / font scaling without truncating or breaking layouts.
Respect prefers-reduced-motion for every animation (already a DESIGN.md rule — enforce it in code).
Never convey meaning by color alone; provide focus states on web; keyboard navigability for the web build.
Media: captions/transcripts where we have them (video updates are transcribed — surface it).

App Store / Play Store requirements (bake in now):

In-app account deletion (Apple requires it; ours is stronger — hard-delete cascade per DATA.md). Must be reachable from Settings.
Privacy manifests / data-safety declarations (Apple privacy nutrition label, Play Data safety) must match what we actually collect — keep them updated as features land.
Permission prompts (camera, mic, contacts, notifications, photo library for the profile-photo exception) each need a clear purpose string, requested in context, never at launch.
Sign in with Apple must be offered since we offer Google sign-in.
Payments: co-op membership is sold in-app. Where the platform requires it, membership uses the platform's own purchase (Apple Pay / Google Pay via IAP); we also plan to support a third-party card processor inside the join flow for cards, and joining is always skippable (a user can decline and keep using the free app). External chip-in handles (Venmo/Cash App) are peer-to-peer text links we never process, and we keep that distinction clean. Current wave uses a "soft join" stub (no real StoreKit/Play/Stripe yet); that is intentional, not a bug.
No ad tracking and no third-party ad SDKs. We DO use a first-party product-analytics tool (PostHog), which is approved. It must stay de-identified, deletable with the account, never sold or fed to matching. Product analytics is on by default while signed in (demo/logged-out stay off). See `analytics-rules.mdc`.
Content safety: report + block exist (FRIENDS.md) — keep them wired; stores require a moderation path for user-generated content.
Make sure to build the accessibility elements in there as you build out the features so that it can be accessed by Android or IOS's accessibility rules. We want to make sure the software is accessible to everyone if they have those options turned on. 
Account & data (additions)
- Deleting an account must also purge it from any third-party processor (e.g. the PostHog person, per analytics-rules.mdc), not just our own DB.
- Account/data export available on request (privacy-law expectation; DATA.md already supports it).
- Ship Apple's Privacy Manifest file. It lives at `apps/mobile/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` (next to `app.json`), declaring "required-reason" APIs and every third-party SDK. Keep it in sync as SDKs (e.g. PostHog) land.
- Don't require account creation to view content the app presents publicly; only ask for signup data we actually need (Apple 5.1.1).

Permissions (additions)
- Location permission (for the coarse "where you met") also needs its own in-context purpose string (not yet exactly applicable until we add local friend finding)
- If contacts are ever used for friend-finding, that requires separate explicit consent + a clear reason, and must be optional -- would like to add this in the onbaording flow if possible when they invite friends they can connect their contacts. 
- The app must fully function when a permission is denied — degrade gracefully, never dead-end.

Payments (addition)
- Do not link out to an external purchase flow for co-op membership in a way that violates Apple 3.1.1 anti-steering. Membership join supports platform purchase (Apple Pay / Google Pay via IAP) and, when we ship it, an in-app third-party card processor; joining stays skippable. Chip-in handles (Venmo/Cash App) are peer-to-peer text links we never process. Keep that line clean. Soft-join stub is fine until real payments land.

Content safety / UGC (additions — required because we have user content + messaging, Apple Guideline 1.2)
- Report the content itself, not just block the person, on stories, messages, inside jokes, and comments.
- A mechanism to filter/remove objectionable content with a stated response time, and a reported-content pipeline that reaches a human (admin console, ADMIN.md).
- An EULA/terms with a "no tolerance for objectionable content or abusive users" clause the user must agree to (Apple 1.2).
- Age gate / minimum age appropriate to a social app, and an accurate age-rating questionnaire.

AI content (addition)
- Because summaries are model-written, ensure they can't emit harmful/objectionable output, and provide a report path on AI-written content too (stores are scrutinizing generative features).

Accessibility (addition — build in per feature, not a later pass)
- As each feature is built, make it work with iOS (VoiceOver, Dynamic Type, Voice Control, Switch Control, Reduce Motion) and Android (TalkBack, font scaling, Switch Access) when users have those enabled — mirroring CURSOR-RULES §3. Do not defer this to an accessibility pass at the end.


4 · Every section of code gets a plain-language comment and every line break gets one sentence introducing the next portion of the code

The founder is non-technical and will read the code. Every file starts with a header comment, and every meaningful section gets a one-liner, in plain English that a kindergartener could understand, put more technical jargon in parenthesis— what it is, not how clever it is.

ts
// ============================================
// WHAT THIS FILE DOES (plain English):
// This is the "Touch Grass" button — the big green button on the Events
// page. Tapping it opens the sheet where you pick who to tell and when.
// (Home does NOT have the send button; it only shows friends' signals to
// answer. If you change the button's look, do it in the Magic Patterns
// component, not here.)
// ============================================

// --- SECURITY: only signed-in users can send a signal ---
// --- THE BUTTON: what the user taps ---
// --- SENDING: tells the server to notify the chosen circle ---
Comments say what and why ("this checks you can only see fields shared with your tier"), not just restating code.
Label the load-bearing bits explicitly: SECURITY, PRIVACY, PAYMENT, ACCESSIBILITY, so they're findable.
Keep comments updated when code changes — a stale comment is worse than none.
In addition! Every line break should have one sentence that says what the next section does -- really simply. // THIS SECTION DOES: ...

5 · Privacy invariants (never break these, in any task)
RLS on every table; tier visibility enforced at the row level (DATA.md). New table → new policy, same migration.
Deletion is real: hard-delete cascades; removing an account/field/connection erases it and its derived AI rows.
AI never sees PII or photos: summaries from user words only; embeddings from de-identified Zone B; matching returns opaque IDs, names rejoin on-device. Never send likeness to a model; never train on user content.
The Assistant is single-user (personal_agent lane) and never acts without an explicit confirm; it must not bleed into Home/Discover for people who have not opted in.
No vanity metrics, anywhere: no follower counts, view counts, invited totals, streaks, or leaderboards. If a feature needs a number, it must be a number that serves the user (host headcount vs cap is the canonical allowed example).
Capture-only media for circle video replies and stickers. Two upload exceptions: the profile photo, and photos/short videos chosen from the camera roll for a Scrapbook page (approved 2026-09-08; permission asked in context, only picked items are read, source is stored per element).

