7 · Working method & definition of done
Before building a feature, read its doc (see INDEX.md for the map) and follow its acceptance criteria — they are the checklist.
**Always review (every task):** open INDEX.md’s “Always review” list. Today that includes:
- `NOTIFICATIONS.md` — if this change can create, show, or deep-link an alert/push, check the destination map and update it in the same change when a kind is missing or wrong.
- `guide-docs/docs/PRIVACY.md` and `guide-docs/docs/TERMS.md` — add anything this change introduces (new data, permission, payment, analytics, AI, UGC, moderation, deletion, age gate).
- `ADMIN.md` § API / integrations health — if you add or change an **outbound** Nest integration (OAuth like Spotify, email/SMS, AI vendor, payment processor, etc.), register a non-secret health check on `GET /admin/integrations/health` (`apps/api/src/admin/integrations-health.service.ts`) in the same change so the admin console **API / integrations** page stays complete. Do not put dependency probes on public `GET /health` (App Runner liveness only).
Small, reviewable changes; migrations reversible; seed data kept working.
A feature is done when: it matches its doc's acceptance criteria · uses Magic Patterns components · passes lint/typecheck/tests · has accessibility labels + reduced-motion respected · has plain-language comments · leaks no secrets client-side · RLS covers any new data · any new notify-able outcome is registered in `NOTIFICATIONS.md` with a tap/push destination · **`PRIVACY.md` / `TERMS.md` reflect anything new about data use or user obligations** · **and any new outbound API is listed on admin integrations health**.
When a doc is ambiguous or two docs conflict: stop and ask. Don't guess on privacy, payments, or permissions.
Doc authority — the feature docs live in the `guide-docs/` folder; this rules file lives at `.cursor/rules/guide-rules.mdc` (highest wins)
`.cursor/rules/guide-rules.mdc` (this file, historically called CURSOR-RULES.md) — how to work
INFRASTRUCTURE.md — stack, hosting, secrets, build order
DATA.md — schema, privacy zones, RLS
ARCHITECTURE.md — repo layout, modules, routes
Feature docs (HOME.md, PROFILE.md, STORIES.md, …) — behavior + acceptance criteria
NOTIFICATIONS.md — always-review destination map for every alert/push (see INDEX.md)
DESIGN.md + MAGIC-PATTERNS.md — look, motion, components
`guide-docs/docs/PRIVACY.md` + `guide-docs/docs/TERMS.md` — living Privacy Policy + Terms / EULA drafts (inform the real legal docs)
`apps/mobile/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` — Apple Privacy Manifest (keep in sync with SDKs)

8 · Living PRIVACY.md and TERMS.md (not optional cleanup)
Paths: `guide-docs/docs/PRIVACY.md` and `guide-docs/docs/TERMS.md`.
These are the **master plain-English drafts** lawyers will turn into the real Privacy Policy and Terms / EULA. They are not marketing copy and not code comments. Keep them accurate and boring.

**When you must edit them (same PR / same change as the code):**
- New or changed data collected, stored, shared, or deleted
- New permission (camera, mic, notifications, contacts, location, photos)
- Analytics / PostHog event families or consent behavior
- Payments, membership, refunds, cancel-at-period-end, chip-in handles
- AI or matching that touches user content or attributes
- UGC surfaces (Updates, Inside Jokes, messages, recap, portal comments) and report / block / moderation
- Account deletion, export, or retention changes
- Age gate / minimum age / EULA acceptance

**How to edit:** add a short bullet under the right scaffold heading. Prefer "what we do / why / who sees it / how long / how to stop" over legal flourish. Mark unknowns as `TODO (legal): …`. Do not invent legal promises the product does not keep. If you touch an already-shipped area and the section is still a stub, fill the stub from current product truth before finishing.
